est to share the main ideas with you  i myself am not an expert in this topic  so it's just what i've been able to pick  up  i would like to quickly thank galaxy  lamps for sponsoring this video the  amazing background that you see today is  from their galaxy projector 2.0 which is  something that you can easily point at  the walls or ceiling to turn any room  into a kind of planetarium it is really  quite mesmerizing and i'd love to keep  using it as a backdrop for future videos  it integrates with an app to change  color options speed and to set a timer  but you can also just use the six  inbuilt modes if you don't want to use  your phone  you can also set it up with amazon alexa  or google assistant it has a three stand  system which is really easy to set up or  rotate and it doesn't make any noise it  has a laser feature which makes the  stars or you can just have the nebula  lights if you would like to get one for  yourself or maybe as a gift perhaps for  christmas then you can head to galaxy  lamps dot co slash tibbies and get a 15  discount that link will only work for  100 people and you can find it down in  the description as well  so looking at the thesis it is all about  the expansion of the universe and the  first line of the introduction is that  the idea that the universe is expanding  is of recent origin  he talks about how all of the early  cosmologies were essentially stationary  including einsteins  but he says there are grave difficulties  associated with this including the fact  that if the stars had been radiating  energy at their present rates for an  infinite time they would need an  infinite supply of energy  and there's a mention of the fact that  not long before this thesis was written  that the discovery of the recession of  the nebulae by hubble had led to the  abandonment of static models in favor of  ones which were expanding  so this expansion is a relatively new  idea and hawking goes on to say there  are a few possibilities  one is that the universe may have  expanded from a highly dense state a  finite time ago which is the big bang  model or there's the bouncing or  oscillating model where the universe is  going through periods of expansion and  contraction finally hawking says it is  possible that the expansion may have  been proceeding at much the same rate  for an infinite time for this it would  be necessary to postulate some form of  continual creation of matter in order to  prevent the expansion from reducing the  density to zero  this is what is called the steady state  model now hawking will go on to talk a  lot more about that it's also important  to establish that a lot of this thesis  relates to einstein's general theory of  relativity the idea that space and time  are related in this kind of fabric  called space-time where massive objects  can warp space-time explaining things  like planetary orbits or a gravitational  pull einstein himself had been so sure  that the universe was static and not  expanding that he had added the  cosmological constant to his equations  in order to keep it that way which he  would famously go on to call his  greatest blunder  chapter one is about the hoyl nalika  theory of gravitation and basically why  it is wrong  fred hoyle was an astronomer at  cambridge and is most famous for his  theory of stellar nucleosynthesis  showing that elements up to iron can be  formed inside stars in nuclear reactions  he also came up with the term big bang  which he used to mock the theory because  he rejected it he was instead a  proponent of the steady state model of  the universe that the universe didn't  come from anything but is infinitely old  and the same in all directions and at  all times in the past and future  a steady state universe doesn't evolve  over time jayant nalika was fred hoyle's  phd student and is still working in  astronomy he's currently based in india  together they came up with this theory  to explain how the universe could be  expanding and still in a steady state as  hawking said in his introduction this  would require some continual creation of  mata so we have here the big bang model  if all of these dots represent galaxies  then with the expansion of the universe  the distance between everything and  between all the galaxies increases then  for the steady-state model the distance  between individual galaxies marked here  does increase by the same amount when  the universe expands but this time there  is extra matter created in between  so the average distance between galaxies  remains the same  hoyl and nalika call this new mata the c  field and interestingly it is based off  work from richard feynman's thesis  feynman had this theory of fields that  were half backwards and half forwards  which was derived from an action  principle that involved interactions  between pairs of particles in feynman's  thesis he was able to show that these  fields could combine to give an observed  field  and hoyl and nalikar used the same  theory for space time you can even see  that they start their theory here by  deriving it from something they call the  action  an integration over the world lines of  particles  funnily enough when hawking came to  cambridge as a student he had actually  wanted hoyle as his own supervisor  and he was a bit upset when he was  placed with sayama but then in this  thesis he's really going after hoyle's  theory so maybe it worked out for the  best  all of this math goes on to show that  the steady state theory doesn't match  observation  he says that it excludes models that  seem to correspond to the actual  universe hawking points out lots of  problems with the theory but  interestingly one of them is that it  would require some particles to have the  property of negative gravitational mass  and he says there does not seem to be  any matter having these properties in  our region of space  and that the introduction of negative  masses would probably raise more  difficulties than it would solve and  this was actually before we had the idea  of dark energy which kind of does have  anti-gravitational or negative mass  properties chapter 2 is about  perturbations these small variations in  space-time  to describe this hawking uses the metric  tensor  and works with the einstein field  equations i've got a link down in the  description to some videos that cover  this math of general relativity in a  much better way that i can here  but all this math is done to show the  conclusion that galaxies cannot form as  the result of the growth of small  perturbations but maybe this is where  hawking got it wrong because these days  we actually believe that that is exactly  how galaxies form however now we have  the additional component of dark matter  providing enough invisible mass to hold  the galaxies together but it wasn't  until 1980 that we had the idea of dark  matter when vera rubin published her  work on galaxy masses and it was around  that same year that dark energy was also  proposed but this thesis was written 15  years earlier  but i suppose statements like this one  from hawking do add to the weight of  reason as to why we needed something  like dark matter because without it  there are a few things that just don't  add up  you can see how differently a thesis was  written without modern tools like latex  and word processes to type out the  equations  in fact hawking's thesis was typed up by  his wife jane while she was also  completing her own graduate studies  no thesis would be complete without some  spelling mistakes that went unnoticed  but i only noticed them now because of a  lack of ability to understand what's  going on with the rest of the page  chapter 3 is gravitational radiation in  an expanding universe  this actually relates to what we now  call gravitational waves they were first  predicted by einstein in 1915 and we  observed them for the first time in  2015.  ligo picked up a signal from a merger of  two black holes that sent gravitational  ripples all the way to earth  hawking mentions them a bit in chapter  two but calls them slightly academic  since gravitational radiation has not  yet been detected let alone investigated  and the treatment in chapter 3 is much  more mathematical if you can believe it  we've got the field equations tenses  metrics this is the math of general  relativity it's about describing how  space moves and if anything it's really  all about geometry  not much of this thesis is hawking just  pondering about ideas for the universe  you can see that really most of it is  about proving those ideas with  mathematics  it's why topics like cosmology and  general relativity are almost less of a  physics discipline and more about  learning the math  often these researchers work within math  departments  of course everything looks strange when  i'm just throwing it in front of you on  a page and it would make a bit more  sense if you're following it through  line by line  like even with just more basic algebra  if someone just showed you a random page  of their working it'd be hard to make  sense of it this math is hard because  not a lot of us are very fluent with  tenses in this kind of way  but that doesn't mean it's impossible to  learn  now i'm glad you're still around because  chapter four is the last chapter and  it's probably the most important it's  about singularities  he talks about open and closed models  for the universe  in an open universe space has negative  curvature and the universe has no bounds  and will expand forever  for a closed universe space has positive  curvature  and is more like the surface of a sphere  there's one other option which is if  space has no curvature at all which is a  flat universe  the evidence we have now makes us  believe that we live in a flat universe  and hawking investigates this using a  mathematical tool called a calci surface  hawking says that a calci surface will  be taken to mean a complete connected  space-like surface that intersects every  time-like and null line once and once  only so i think it's some kind of slice  through space time and it represents an  instant in time  for a closed universe with positive  curvature the calcium surface is said to  be compact whereas the non-compact  surface represents an open universe or  one with negative curvature  at this time the mathematician roger  penrose had a theory of a space-time  singularity in the centre of black holes  and hawking in his thesis is applying  this idea to not just black holes but  the entire universe  his bold claims about singularities  include saying that penrose's methods  are applicable to  non-compact calci surfaces  that is universes which have negative or  zero curvature  that is open or flat  it means hawking is really proving that  singularities can exist and are in fact  essential for our universe  that there is some infinitely small and  dense point at the beginning of  space-time and he's not just saying it  he can actually prove it and here's his  proof right here  in fact it is a proof by contradiction  he starts with saying  assume space-time is singularity-free  doing this he encounters contradictions  his contradictions are that the universe  would be both open and closed at the  same time he says this is a  contradiction thus the assumption that  space-time is non-singular must be false  later hawking would go on to work quite  closely with roger penrose and extend  these theories of singularities they  published a 1970 proof that the universe  must have begun as a singularity if it  is to obey the general theory of  relativity  it was these mathematical proofs of  singularities and of the big bang that  hawking first became quite well known  for  he then moved on to working with black  holes and showed that they can emit what  is now called hawking radiation  and later he became involved with  quantum mechanics and thinking about  ways to unite it with general relativity  when hawking released his thesis to the  public he said that by making my phd  thesis open access  i hope that i inspire people to look up  at the stars and not down at their feet  to wonder about our place in the  universe and to try to make sense of the  cosmos  when he later heard that so many people  had tried to download it he said that he  hoped they were not disappointed by it  i also hope that you gained some value  in what i was able to share with you  thanks for watching thanks also to my  patreon supporters including today's  patreon cat of the day halloween  [Music]  you

